config WTK_GT917S_E977
	bool "Goodix GT917S touch for the MediaTek package (Doogee S88 Pro, from the disassembly)"
	default n
	depends on !TOUCHSCREEN_MTK_GT1151 && !TOUCHSCREEN_MTK_GT5688
	help
	  Goodix GT917S touchscreen of the Doogee S88 Pro, reconstructed by
	  reading the disassembled factory kernel.  The factory config turns it
	  on (CONFIG_WTK_GT917S_E977=y at line 2220 of
	  e977_dg_m13_71_q0.config) and the ALPS tree does not have the symbol:
	  without this entry `olddefconfig` discards the factory config line in
	  silence.

	  THE `depends on` IS NOT A PRECAUTION, IT IS A MEASUREMENT.  The three
	  Goodix variants define the SAME global symbols:

	    $ comm -12 <(nm --defined-only --extern-only GT917S/built-in.o) \
	                <(nm --defined-only --extern-only GT5688/built-in.o)
	    92 names, among them gt1x_i2c_read, gt1x_init, gesture_event_handler
	    (91 with GT1151/)

	  Turning two of them on together makes the link fail with "multiple
	  definition".  The factory indeed keeps both off (lines 2210 and 2216
	  of the same config), and this line makes it impossible instead of
	  leaving it to whoever configures.

	  THERE IS NO SECOND `depends on`, and an earlier draft had a wrong one:
	  it excluded ILITEK and FT8719 "because all three register a
	  tpd_driver_t for the same panel".  The factory config turns all three
	  on (lines 2220, 2224, 2227), and MediaTek's `tpd` framework chooses at
	  run time.  The three do not even have one global symbol in common --
	  measured, zero -- so there is nothing to exclude.  The constraint that
	  remains is only the Goodix one above, which is a real collision.

	  The factory block has 85 functions in six translation units, and 84
	  match to the byte with the factory compiler (clang-r353983c).  The only
	  one that differs is gt1x_touch_event_handler, +8.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE `GTP_*` SYMBOLS, AND WHY THEY ARE HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GT917S did not build from the config at all, and the first real link found out:
#
#   gt1x_generic.c:990:7: error: use of undeclared identifier 'gesture_enabled'
#   gt1x_generic.c:2216:6: error: use of undeclared identifier 'hotknot_enabled'
#   gt1x_generic.c:2408:19: error: use of undeclared identifier 'GTP_INT_TRIGGER'
#
# The sources use those names, and `include/gt1x_tpd_common.h` declares them only
# under CONFIG_GTP_GESTURE_WAKEUP, CONFIG_GTP_HOTKNOT and CONFIG_GTP_CUSTOM_CFG.
# Those symbols, however, lived INSIDE `if TOUCHSCREEN_MTK_GT5688` in GT5688's
# Kconfig: they exist only if GT5688 is on, and the factory keeps it off.
#
# IT IS A DEFECT OF MEASUREMENT, NOT ONLY OF WIRING. The "84 functions out of 85
# equal to the byte" for GT917S was measured in a tree whose `.config` had
# CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_MTK_GT5688=y -- that is, in a configuration that is NOT the
# factory one, and from which the block borrowed its GTP_*.
# The defaults below reproduce exactly the ones the measurement was made with,
# so that the number stays reproducible without GT5688:
#
#   CONFIG_GTP_CUSTOM_CFG=y      CONFIG_GTP_AUTO_UPDATE=y
#   CONFIG_GTP_GESTURE_WAKEUP=y  CONFIG_GTP_CREATE_WR_NODE=y
#   CONFIG_GTP_HOTKNOT=y         (all the others: n)
#
# Kconfig allows the same symbol to be defined in more than one file -- the entries
# merge -- and the two `if`s are mutually exclusive by construction.

if WTK_GT917S_E977
config GT917S_FIRMWARE
	string "GT917S per MediaTek firmware"

